The array is split into two sorted parts. Pairs inside each part are counted recursively. Before merging, a pointer walks through the right part and counts values that are less than half of each left value. The pointer only moves forward because both parts are sorted.

Dry run for `[1, 3, 2, 3, 1]`: the right side `[3, 1]` gives one pair when `3 > 2 * 1`. At the final merge, the left value `3` also matches the right value `1`, giving the answer `2`.

The comparison uses `2L` so doubling an integer cannot overflow. Merge sort writes sorted values back into `nums`, so the input array is changed.

```java
class Solution {
    public int reversePairs(int[] nums) {
        return sortAndCount(nums, 0, nums.length - 1);
    }

    private int sortAndCount(int[] nums, int left, int right) {
        if (left >= right) {
            return 0;
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
        int count = sortAndCount(nums, left, middle);
        count += sortAndCount(nums, middle + 1, right);

        int scan = middle + 1;
        for (int i = left; i <= middle; i++) {
            while (scan <= right && (long) nums[i] > 2L * nums[scan]) {
                scan++;
            }
            count += scan - (middle + 1);
        }

        merge(nums, left, middle, right);
        return count;
    }

    private void merge(int[] nums, int left, int middle, int right) {
        int[] merged = new int[right - left + 1];
        int i = left;
        int j = middle + 1;
        int index = 0;

        while (i <= middle && j <= right) {
            if (nums[i] <= nums[j]) {
                merged[index++] = nums[i++];
            } else {
                merged[index++] = nums[j++];
            }
        }

        while (i <= middle) {
            merged[index++] = nums[i++];
        }
        while (j <= right) {
            merged[index++] = nums[j++];
        }

        for (int k = 0; k < merged.length; k++) {
            nums[left + k] = merged[k];
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the merge array and recursion.

Kept this one direct. For every index, the code checks all positions after it and counts the pair when the left value is more than twice the right value. Python integers do not overflow here, so the comparison stays simple.

For `nums = [1, 3, 2, 3, 1]`, the valid pairs are `(3, 1)` at indices `(1, 4)` and `(3, 4)`, so the result is `2`.

